# Gantry

## Overview

Gantry is a crane-building puzzle, played in the browser in a 3D construction
yard. Each site sets out anchor points, a handful of loads, and the pads they
must be delivered to. The player builds a crane out of struts, cables, and rails
on a lattice, mounts a slew ring and a trolley track, then writes an instruction
tape that drives the crane's four axes: slewing the arm, driving the trolley,
hoisting the cable, and turning the grip. Running the tape plays it out under a
structural simulation: every member carries a computed force, an overloaded
member breaks, and a load swings on its cable as the crane moves it.

A site is cleared when every load has been set down on its pad. The score is the
crane's cost and the tape's running time, so a cheap crane driven briskly beats
an overbuilt one driven timidly. Driving briskly has a price: moving faster
loads the structure harder and swings the load wider, so speed is bought with
steel.

Gantry has its own name, look, and rules, and its assets, branding, and design
are original to it.

The specification spans several files under `specs/`. `specs/world.md` gives the
world frame, the lattice, and the anatomy of a site; `specs/structure.md` the
members, materials, and parts a crane is built from and the rules the editor
enforces; `specs/statics.md` how member forces are computed and how the
structure fails; `specs/rigging.md` the hoist cable, the hook, the swinging
load, and attaching and releasing; `specs/program.md` the instruction tape and
the axis motion it commands; `specs/controls.md` the camera, the pointer, and
the key actions; `specs/state.md` what the game's state carries;
`specs/instrumentation.md` the debug and automation surface; `specs/ui.md` the
screens, the readouts, and the audio; `specs/sites.md` the fixed set of sites;
and `specs/assets.md` the assets the build produces and ships. Read all of them
and build the game they describe as one cohesive whole.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The runtime

This game stands on no engine, so the runtime under it is yours to write: the
frame loop and the delta time it measures, the canvas fit, input from both the
keyboard and the pointer, audio cues, and the debug overlay. Where this
specification speaks of the runtime it means that layer, and it states only what
Gantry needs from it. How the layer is structured is yours to decide.

Rendering is also yours. The project ships `three` as a dependency for the 3D
scene and `@test-cabinet/voxel-runtime` for loading the models the build
produces; how the scene is put together on top of them is your design.
{{else}}
## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It owns the frame loop and
the delta time the game is given, the input actions the menus and camera are
driven by, the pointer, audio cues, asset loading, the canvas fit, and the debug
overlay. Read its documentation before you start. This specification states only
what is specific to Gantry.

Rendering is the game's. The project ships `three` as a dependency for the 3D
scene and `@test-cabinet/voxel-runtime` for loading the models the build
produces; how the scene is put together on top of them is your design. The
pointer reaches the game from the engine in the stage's logical units, with its
press and release edges; `specs/controls.md` states what Gantry does with them.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested
with Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain is
fixed and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`. The directory does not exist yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is where
your build starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop and the
canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio, the diagnostics overlay, the
debug and automation surface, the figures this specification fixes, and the game
itself.

The game's state is yours to shape. `specs/state.md` states what it must carry,
and `specs/instrumentation.md` states the one contract fixed over it.

### What stays as it is

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the stage is fitted into.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need, added
along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and scripts stay
as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, identifier, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is stated here under a name. Name each one once in a module
of your own and read it from there, rather than restating a value at each use.
Anything this specification does not name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured
over `src/`. A test drives the game over its own clock for a counted number of
ticks, so it needs no browser and no WebGL. Cover the parts of the game a person
cannot see at a glance: the placement rules that accept or refuse an edit, the
solve and the member forces it reports, the breakage cascade, the axis motion
profiles, the pendulum update, and the attach and release verdicts.
{{else}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, and any new modules you add beside it under `src/`.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<GantryState, GantryDebugApi>`:
`initialize` builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them
together as `[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a read-only
view (`DeepReadonly<GantryState>`) and returns the next state, advanced against
the frame's delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed that next state,
read-only again, and draws it. The engine holds the state by value and replaces
it with what each `update` returns, so a frame builds the next state from the
current one rather than writing into it, and nothing but `update` and the debug
surface's poses advances the game. All three functions throw `"not implemented"`
as they stand. Implement them, and split the work across new files under `src/`
however suits you.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `GantryState` and the types it is built
from, exactly as `specs/state.md` states them, and `GantryDebugApi`, the debug
and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the canvas is
cleared to each frame, so the letterbox bars around the stage match the yard's
sky. The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color your yard
uses.

