### The checks the repository passes

Four commands run over the finished repository before it is reviewed:

| Command | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | The code type-checks. A repository that does not compile is not reviewed. |
| `npx eslint .` | The code is lint-clean. |
| `npx prettier --check .` | The code is formatted. |
| `npx vitest run --coverage` | Your tests pass, with their count and coverage reported. |

They run on the repository as you leave it. `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`,
`npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served as-is at
the root of any static file server, and equally when served from a sub-path, so
every asset reference in the build is relative rather than root-absolute. The
scripts that do this are already in `package.json`.

The build is self-contained. The asset tools on this machine's `PATH` are
development tools for producing the files under `assets/`; the produced files
are committed, the build bundles them, and neither `npm ci` nor `npm run build`
invokes a tool. `specs/assets.md` states what is produced.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser.
This is a substantial build: a 3D editor with pointer picking on a lattice, a
structural solver that runs on every simulation tick, an instruction tape and
the choreography it drives, a swinging load, six authored sites, several screens
and menus, and a produced set of models and sounds. Aim for a game a person
would enjoy playing, where a collapse is legible and a clean lift is
satisfying.

### Hard requirements

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Write the runtime the game stands on: the frame loop and its delta time, the
  canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio, and the debug overlay.
{{else}}
- Build on the {{engine.name}} engine, taking the frame loop, the input
  actions, the pointer, audio, and the debug overlay from it.
{{/if}}
- Ship all six sites, reachable in order from the site select.
- Render a real 3D scene on the canvas: the yard, the lattice aids, the crane's
  members and parts, the loads, and the readouts, with a camera the player
  orbits.
- Ship the produced models and audio `specs/assets.md` lists, wired in as that
  file states, and draw everything else in code. The build fetches nothing at
  runtime from outside its own `dist/`.
- Run in the browser with no backend. Everything needed to play is
  self-contained, and no API key or credential is needed to build, run, or play.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- Hold the game's whole state in one value, the one the frame loop advances and
  the debug surface reads.
{{else}}
- Hold the game's whole state in `GantryState`, as `specs/state.md` declares it.
{{/if}}
- Replace the starter `README.md` with one explaining what the game is, how to
  install dependencies, how to run it in development, how to produce the static
  production build, and the controls.

## Code quality

The code is written as for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers.

- Modules are split by concern, and each has one responsibility.
- Every fixed figure is named once and imported where it is used.
- Logic appears once; no two places implement the same rule.
- The tree carries no dead code, no commented-out code, and no leftover
  debugging output.
- Comments explain intent where the code cannot.
- The type-check, lint, and format checks pass.
- Unit tests cover the simulation.

## Units, ticks, and the stage

The world is measured in the game's own units on a right-handed frame: `x` and
`z` are horizontal, `y` is up, and the ground is the plane `y = 0`. Angles this
specification states are in degrees, masses in mass units, and forces in force
units, with weight being mass times `GRAVITY` (`10`) units per second squared.
`specs/world.md` states the frame in full.

The simulation advances on a fixed tick of `TICK_HZ` (`60`) ticks per simulated
second, and every run of a tape is the same sequence of ticks whatever the frame
rate: each frame's delta time accumulates, whole ticks are consumed from the
accumulation, and a remainder shorter than a tick waits for the next frame.
Outside a run nothing ticks; menus and the editor respond to input as it
arrives, and the camera moves against the frame's delta time.

The canvas presents a fixed logical stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`,
16:9). Fitting it to the browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that
preserves the aspect ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel
ratio. The complete stage is on screen at every window size, on load and at any
pixel density. The 3D viewport fills the stage, screen-space readouts are laid
out in logical stage units, and the pointer position the game reads is in those
same units.

## Visual design

The yard is yours to art-direct: the palette, the sky, the ground, the light,
and the styling of every screen. Legibility is gameplay, so whatever look you
choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| The lattice | On the build screen, the buildable lattice and the envelope's extent are visible aids, and the picked node under the pointer is highlighted. |
| Anchors and pads | Anchor points, each load's starting position, and each pad's footprint and required yaw are marked so a site is readable before anything is built. |
| Members by material | A strut, a cable, and a rail are told apart at a glance, by form and not by hue alone. |
| Utilization | While the tape runs, each member's color reads its utilization on a monotone ramp from slack to its limit, a member at breaking point stands out, and a broken member is unmistakable. |
| The load | An attached load visibly hangs from the hook on its cable, and its swing is drawn true to the simulation. |
| Readouts | The axis values, the run clock, the cost, and the failure cause when a run fails are legible against the scene. |
| Text | Text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The produced models and sounds carry the rest of the look;
`specs/assets.md` holds them to the same bar.
